er knows no bounds,\nwho willingly give their bodies over to possession. All take part\nin the blood sacrifices, dark rituals, and worship of Daemons –\n\n[Page 94]\nMutations\nThose who dwell in Mordheim soon develop horrible mutations, and the Cult of the Possessed seem to be especially susceptible. In\naddition, Mordheim attracts mutants from all over the Empire, who are always quick to join the Chaos covens. Most mutations are\nsimply inconvenient or hideous, but some make their bearers extremely dangerous in combat.\n\nMUTATION\nA warrior with this special rule must purchase a Mutation when they are recruited. Possessed Warriors must take 2 Mutations.\nYou may not buy new mutations for a model after recruitment. A warrior may only have one Mutation and it is rare for a\nMutation to be duplicated within the same warband. The nature of Chaos is never consistent. To represent this, the first warrior to\ntake a Mutation pays the cost shown for it. The second warrior to take the same Mutation pays double the cost shown, the third\none to take the same Mutation pays triple the cost shown and so on.\n    • Mutations cannot be disarmed.\n    • Mutations do not count as a weapon when equipping warriors.\n\n\ndaemon soul                                                         blackblood\nCOST: 25 GOLD CROWNS.                                               COST: 30 GOLD CROWNS\nA Daemon lives within the warrior’s body. The warrior gains         If the warrior loses a wound in close combat, any warrior in\nthe special rule Magic Resistance (4+). Note, this doesn’t dispel   base contact (friend or foe) with the model suffers a Strength 3\nthe spell, which may still affect other warriors.                   hit from the spurting corrosive blood. Make armour saves as\n                                                                    normal.\ngreat claw\nCOST: 40 GOLD CROWNS.                                               spines\nOne of the warrior’s arms ends in a huge claw, t