ants offering their goods and services. Small settlements       items. Common items can be bought quite readily in any of the\nare served by travelling traders.                                    numerous trading posts and settlements around Mordheim.\n                                                                     Players may purchase as many of these items as they want. The\nAll warbands are able to get new equipment one way or                price of common items is fixed, so players always pay the same\nanother. The most unscrupulous merchants actively trade with         rate for them.\nthe Possessed Cultists, and creatures such as the Skaven have        Rare items are hard or even impossible to come by. Only\ntheir own network of contacts, while Vampires can send the           occasionally do such items turn up for sale and the price asked\nDregs that serve them into the less salubrious settlements           is often way above the true value. These items are often offered\naround Mordheim, such as Cutthroats’ Haven.                          only to the most famous warbands, or those with the most\n                                                                     money.\nspending cash\nAfter every game, a warband can collect income from                  availability\nexploration, scenario rewards, etc, and sell any wyrdstone and       The Price chart has a column marked ‘Availability’. Common\ntreasures they have acquired. Cash can be spent on recruiting        items are always available, and can be bought in any quantity.\nnew warriors, or on new equipment for the warband. The price         Items marked ‘rare’ are more difficult to find. A rare item’s\nof wyrdstone varies according to current demand. See the             availability is indicated by a number, for example ‘Rare 9’.\nIncome section for details.                                          Whenever a Hero wants to buy a rare item, roll 2D6 and\n                                                                     compare the result to the number stated. If the roll is equal or\nnew recruits                                                         greater, the item is available. For example, you need to roll 9 or\n                                                                     more to acquire an item marked ‘Rare 9’. You can only buy\nNew warriors are recruited in the same way as the original           one rare item for each successful roll. You may also only make\nwarband with the notable exception of equipment. Warbands            one roll for each Hero looking for rare items. For example, if\nmay recruit whatever type of warriors the player wishes, but         your warband has four Heroes, you may make four rolls to\nthe usual restrictions apply regarding the number of Heroes,         locate rare items. Warriors taken out of action during the last\nHenchmen, wizards, etc. For example, no warband may recruit          battle may not look for rare items.\na second leader.\nRECRUITING HIRED SWORDS\nPlayers may hire mercenary warriors known as ‘Hired Swords’\n                                                                     selling\nfor the warband if they wish. See the Hired Swords section.          A player may trade in weapons and equipment at the same\n                                                                     time he buys new ones. After all, as warbands become more\nweapons                                                              powerful they often abandon their earlier armament in favour\n                                                                     of something better. However, the second-hand value of\nIf a player wants to buy new weapons or other equipment for          equipment is not high due to the considerable wear and tear\nexisting warriors, refer to the Price chart that follows. The        inflicted on it by your warriors.\nchart lists all the equipment available in Mordheim, not just        Warriors can automatically sell equipment for half its listed\nthe common weapons included in the Recruitment charts.               price. In the case of rare equipment and weapons which have a\nRare items and weapons are not always available and vary in          variable price, the warband receives half of the basic cost only\nprice. Remember that your warriors lack the skill to use any         (merchants are far better at haggling than your warriors).\nweapons other than the ones listed in the Recruitment charts.        Alternatively, weapons, armour and equipment may be\nYou may still want to buy rare items offered to you, as your         hoarded for future use (make a note on the warband roster).\nwarriors may be able to use them as they gain in experience.         As a weapon’s value is low compared to the cost of equipping\nPlayers should preferably complete any recruiting and trading        any new recruits you may get, a warband can usually find a use\nafter the battle is over, making the appropriate dice rolls whilst   for its cast-off armaments.\nboth players are present."},{"page":148,"text":"Price chart\nThe following chart indicates the cost of items available for sale at trading posts. The cost of rare items is included, but such items\ncannot be bought unless they are available, as already described.\n\n             CLOSE COMBAT WEAPONS                                                        MISSILE WEAPONS\nItem                          Cost gc        Availability             Item                          Cost gc        Availability\nAssassin’s Blades               25           Rare 9                   Bow                             15           Common\nAxe                             20           Common                   Crossbow                        30           Common\nBastard Sword                   35           Rare 8                   Crossbow pistol                 40           Rare 9\nBillhook                        30           Rare 7                   Dwarf crossbow                  35           Rare 9\nClub                            15           Common                   Elf bow                         50           Rare 12\nDagger                          10           Common                   Heavy crossbow                  45           Rare 10\nFlail                           35           Rare 6                   Long bow                        20           Rare 7\nFlaming Torch                   25           Common                   Net                             15           Rare 9\nGreat Axe                       55           Rare 8                   Repeater crossbow               45           Rare 8\nGreat Hammer                    50           Rare 8                   Short bow                       10           Common\nGreat Sword                     45           Rare 9                   Sling                            5           Rare 6\nGromril Axe                     40           Rare 11                  Throwing axe                    15           Rare 8\nGromril Great Axe               75           Rare 12                  Throwing knives                 15           Rare 8\nHalberd                         30           Rare 6\nHammer or Mace                  20           Common                                BLACKPOWDER WEAPONS\nIthilmar Dagger                 20           Rare 11                  Item                            Cost gc         Availability\nIthilmar Sword                  50           Rare 12                  Blunderbuss                       30            Rare 9\nMorning Star                    30           Rare 8                   Bomb                              50            Rare 10\nPickaxe                         40           Rare 9                   Duelling Pistol                   40            Rare 11\nRapier                          35           Rare 9                   Dwarf Handgun                     55            Rare 11\nSpear                           20           Common                   Dwarf Pistol                      35            Rare 10\nStaff                           25           Rare 6                   Cathay Smoke bombs                40            Rare 12\nSword                           25           Common                   Handgun                           45            Rare 9\nWhip                            30           Rare 9                   Hochland long rifle              100            Rare 12\n                                                                      Pistol                            25            Rare 8\n                          ARMOUR\nItem                          Cost gc         Availability                          PARRYING AND SHIELDS\nChaos armour                   100            Rare 12                 Item                          Cost gc        Availability\nFull plate armour               80            Rare 10                 Buckler                         10           Rare 7\nFur cloak                       15            Rare 8                  Left hand dagger                20           Rare 8\nGromril plate                  120            Rare 11                 Shield                          20           Common\nIthilmar                        90            Rare 11                 Sword breaker                   30           Rare 10\nHeavy armour                    50            Common\nLight armour                    20            Common"},{"page":149,"text":"Price chart (continued)\n\n                       EQUIPMENT                                          ANIMALS\nItem                       Cost gc     Availability   Item                  Cost gc    Availability\nAmulet of Protection         30        Rare 9         Trained bear            60       Rare 10\nAtheryric Repository         50        Rare 11        Wardog                  35       Rare 8\nBlessed water                25        Rare 8         Warhorse                80       Rare 11\nBodkin arrows                10        Rare 8         Wizards familiar       100       Rare 11\nChain cilice                 25        Rare 10\nConcealed blade              10        Rare 5                 POISONS. POTIONS AND DRUGS\nDispel scroll                50        Rare 11        Item                  Cost gc   Availability\nElven cloak                  50        Rare 10        Apothecary kit          30      Rare 9\nGarlic                        5        Rare 5         Black lotus             20      Rare 9\nHoly tome                   140        Rare 10        Bottle of rotgut        15      Common\nHunting arrows               10        Rare 8         Bugman’s ale            30      Rare 9\nImpressive cloak             50        Rare 9         Crimson shade           30      Rare 8\nLantern                      40        Common         Dark venom              40      Rare 9\nLucky charm                  25        Rare 7         Essence of Garlic       10      Rare 6\nMan trap                     20        Rare 6         Healing draught         30      Rare 8\nMordheim map                 60        Rare 9         Laudanum                20      Rare 6\nOpulent jewellery            80        Rare 11        Mad cap mushrooms       40      Rare 9\nReligious symbol             35        Rare 8         Mandrake root           35      Rare 8\nRope and hook                30        Common         Tears of Shallaya       20      Rare 7\nSacred relic                 50        Rare 10\nSuperior Blackpowder         20        Rare 11\nTome of magic               250        Rare 12\nWar horn                     60        Rare 8\nWar trophies                 40        Rare 10\nWyrdstone pendulum           40        Rare 9"},{"page":150,"text":"Hired Swords\n                                                                        require his services, you don’t have to pay any upkeep! These\n              recruiting hired swords                                   costs are indicated in the entries for each Hired Sword. The\nThis section introduces Hired Swords – professional                     money paid to Hired Swords comes from the warband’s\nmercenaries – to Mordheim campaign games. Taverns in the                treasury in the same way as buying new weapons or recruiting\nsettlements and shanty towns around Mordheim are good                   new warriors. If you don’t have enough gold to pay for the\nrecruitment centres for warriors who do not belong to any               Hired Sword, or want to spend it on other things, he leaves the\nparticular warband or retinue, but instead hire out their               warband. Any experience he has gained will be lost, even if you\nservices to the highest bidder. A player can recruit Hired              hire a new Hired Sword of the same type.\nSwords when he creates his warband, or during the campaign\nphase after a game. Hired Swords do not count towards the\nmaximum number of warriors or Heroes a warband may have\n                                                                        injuries\non its roster and don’t affect your income from selling                 If a Hired Sword goes out of action during the game, roll on\nwyrdstone. However, Hired Swords do count as part of the                the Lasting Injuries Table as you would for any warrior.\nwarband for purposes of Rout tests, etc whilst in battle. A\nplayer cannot buy extra weapons or equipment for a Hired\nSword, and he cannot sell the Hired Sword’s weapons or\n                                                                        hired swords and experience\nequipment. To reflect their rarity, you can only have one of            Hired Swords gain experience in the same way as Veterans.\neach type of Hired Sword in your warband.                               Refer to the scenarios to find out how much experience Hired\nYou may not use the Leadership of any of the Hired Swords for           Swords gain after each game. Write the name and profile of a\nRout tests.                                                             Hired Sword on your roster sheet. Once the Hired Sword gains\n                                                                        enough experience for an advance, roll on the Veteran\n                                                                        Advancement table (as opposed to Henchmen) to determine\n                            hire fee                                    which advance they gain. Skills available to the Hired Swords\nWhen a warband recruits a Hired Sword, you must pay his hire            are listed under their entries. They cannot be promoted to a\nfee. Subsequently, after each battle he fights, including the first,    Champion, if this result is rolled then roll for a random skill on\nyou must pay his upkeep fee if you want him to remain with              any table instead.\nthe warband. If the Hired Sword is killed, or you no longer\n\n                                                                       MAY BE HIRED BY\n                       Mercenaries      Witch          Sisters of      The       Skaven             The           Orcs          Dwarfs\n                                        Hunters        Sigmar          Possessed                    Undead\nAssassin                                                                         No                               No\nBounty hunter                                                                    No                               No\nDuellist                                                                         No                 No            No\nDwarf Prospector                                                       No        No                 No            No\nDwarf trollslayer                                      No              No        No                 No            No\nElf ranger                              No                             No        No                 No            No            No\nFallen Knight                                                          No        No                 No            No\nRobber Knight                                          No                        No                               No            No\nGoblin sneak           No               No             No                                                                       No\nHalfling scout                                                         No            No             No            No\nHalfling thief                                                                       No                           No\nMercenary wizard                        No             No\nMinstrel                                                               No            No             No            No\nOgre Bodyguard                                                                       No\nPit warrior                                                                          No             No            No\nTilean marksman                                                                      No             No            No"},{"page":151,"text":"Assassin                                                   Bounty Hunter\n40 gold crowns to hire +20 gold crowns upkeep.                      40 gold crowns to hire +15 gold crowns upkeep.\n\nAssassins live by their wits and their muscles and are not          Villains and outlaws are rife in the Old World. In Mordheim\ngenerally fussy about the sort of work they take on. When           they are as ubiquitous as the ruins that litter the streets. It is\nsomeone needs 'obstacles' removed with discretion, Assassins        the false perception of some outlaws that the depravity and\nare the ones they turn to, and those with the worst reputation      chaos within the city's walls can offer some anonymity from\nget the best jobs. Some Assassins have their own code of what       those men who would seek to bring them to justice and claim\nthey will or won’t do; or are part of a guild, others care for      the price on their heads. Not so, for Bounty Hunters are\nnothing but the coin. Some are simply cut throats; others           determined and resourceful men who will often hire themselves\ncalmly dispatch their rather distasteful duties with                out as mercenaries to roaming warbands in the hope of getting\nfastidiousness and finesse. The Assassin will hire himself out to   closer to their mark. Their mission is to capture at all costs and\nthe highest bidder and satisfaction is guaranteed.                  a little thing like a cursed city isn't even going to slow their\n                                                                    stride...\nMay be Hired: May be hired by any warband except, Skaven\nand Orcs.                                                           May be Hired: May be hired by any warband except, Skaven\n                                                                    and Orcs.\nRating: An Assassin increases the warband’s rating by +22\npoints plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.               Rating: A Bounty Hunter increases the warband’s rating by\n                                                                    +20 points plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.\n M    WS     BS   S   T    W    I   A   LD    CL    WIL     INT\n 4     4     4    3   3    1    5   2    7     8      7      7       M    WS     BS   S   T    W    I   A    LD    CL    WIL    INT\n                                                                     4     4     3    3    4   1    3   1     7     8     8       7\nWEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:\nAll the Assassin’s weapons are coated in Black Lotus poison.        WEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:\nChoose three of the following options:                              Heavy armour, Rope and Hook, Lantern or Flaming Torch\n     • Assassins’ blades                                            Plus choose three of the following options:\n     • Crossbow Pistol                                                  • Sword, Axe, Club or Hammer/mace\n     • Throwing Knives                                                  • Crossbow\n     • Sword                                                            • Pistol\n                                                                        • Net\nSPECIAL RULES:                                                          • Whip\nRace (Human), Hired Sword\n                                                                    SPECIAL RULES:\nSTARTING SKILL:                                                     Race (Human), Hired Sword, Capture (when rolling to see if\nWhen recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Backstab             an enemy warrior is captured a warband with a Bounty\nskill plus one random skill from their Primary skill sets.          Hunter adds +1 to the result).\nSKILL ALLOCATION:\nCunning (Primary), Agility (Secondary), Shooting                    STARTING SKILL:\n(Secondary)                                                         When recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Disarm skill\n                                                                    plus one random skill from their Primary skill sets.\n                                                                    SKILL ALLOCATION:\n                                                                    Shooting (Primary), Cunning (Secondary), Combat\n                                                                    (Secondary)"},{"page":152,"text":"Duellist                                                 Dwarf prospector\n35 gold crowns to hire +15 gold crowns upkeep.                      20 gold crowns to hire +10 gold crowns upkeep.\n\nDuellists fight on behalf of others — settling matters of honour    Many prospectors are tempted by stories of gold in the Skaag\nbetween organisations or individuals — or as surrogates for the     Hills, but real mining is hard work down dark, dangerous\nlaw, representing the accused or the accuser in trials by           tunnels. They are notoriously tough, both physically and\ncombat. For some Duellists the act of fighting is reward enough     mentally. To Dwarfs, mining is not an occupation limited to\nfor the considerable risks they face. Training is dangerous,        commoners. Rather, they are considered artisans of great skill\nleaving some apprentices crippled or scarred for life. Some         and are well-respected amongst the Dawi. Possessed of a keen\nmodern Duellists, especially hot-headed Altdorf students,           sense for stone, Dwarfs are drawn to valuable seams and\nfavour pistols. This is regarded by the older generation as         seemingly have a sixth-sense for when to shore up passages.\ndishonourable and foolhardy.                                        Some Dwarf clans hold such pride in their mining ability that\nBoth inexperienced and esteemed Duellists alike travel the          they march to war armed with picks rather than axes.\nEmpire in search of opponents with which to bolster their           Dwarf Prospectors are specialists in finding riches that other\nreputations. For others, the hunt for a veteran instructor drives   people have tried to hide and, as such, can add considerably to\nthem to explore the furthest flung of the Old World’s locales. As   a warband s purse.\nskilled combatants, Duellists often augment their livelihood\nwith mercenary work, even acting as guards for caravans and         May be Hired: Mercenaries, Sisters of Sigmar, Witch Hunters\nriverboats in a pinch.                                              and Dwarf Clansmen may hire a Dwarf Prospector.\n\nMay be Hired: May be hired by any warband except, Skaven,           Rating: A Dwarf Prospector increases the warband’s rating\nOrcs and Undead.                                                    by +12 points plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.\n\nRating: A Duellist increases the warband’s rating by +18             M    WS    BS    S   T   W    I   A   LD    CL    WIL    INT\npoints plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.                3     4     3    4   4   1    2   1    9     9     9       7\n\n M    WS    BS    S   T   W     I   A   LD    CL    WIL    INT      WEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:\n 4     4     3    3   3    1   4    2    7     7     7       7      Pickaxe, Light Armour, Lantern or Flaming Torch\n\nWEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:                                              SPECIAL RULES:\nRapier, Duelling Pistol                                             Race (Dwarf), Hired Sword,\nPlus choose one of the following options:\n    • Buckler                                                       STARTING SKILL:\n    • Main Gauche                                                   When recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Wyrdstone\n    • Sword Breaker                                                 Hunter skill plus one random skill from their Primary skill\n    • Duelling Pistol (this becomes a pair)                         sets.\n                                                                    SKILL ALLOCATION:\nSPECIAL RULES:                                                      Brawn (Primary), Ferocity (Secondary), Academic\nRace (Human), Hired Sword                                           (Secondary)\n\nSTARTING SKILL:\nWhen recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Rain of\nBlows skill plus one random skill from their Primary skill\nsets.\nSKILL ALLOCATION:\nCombat (Primary), Agility (Secondary), Shooting\n(Secondary)"},{"page":153,"text":"Dwarf Troll Slayer                                                              Elf Ranger\n30 gold crowns to hire +15 gold crowns upkeep.                     40 gold crowns to hire +20 gold crowns upkeep.\n\nTroll Slayers are members of the morbid Dwarf cult whose           Elves are a reclusive race, for the most part they are feared and\nfollowers are obsessed with seeking an honourable death in         distrusted by humans, though some live in the cities amongst\ncombat. Having committed some unforgivable crime or been           men and offer their services as minstrels and archers in return\ndishonoured in an irredeemable way, a Dwarf will forsake his       for a high fee. Though Elves become rarer in the Old World\nhome and wander off to die fighting the enemies of Dwarfkind.      each year, there are still some roaming on the trackless paths of\nTroll Slayers are insanely dangerous individuals, psychopathic     the Drakwald Forest and the Forest of Shadows.\nand violent. However, there are few better warriors, so they are   Elves sensibly tend to avoid the ruins of Mordheim, for in the\nmuch sought after when warriors are needed. Known as ‘Hired        City of the Damned there is little to attract that fey and strange\nAxes’, Troll Slayers who come to Mordheim find plenty of           race, but sometimes they are hired by treasure hunters, for few\nopportunity to indulge their deathwish.                            can match their skill with a bow, or their inhuman quickness\n                                                                   and agility. The senses of an Elf are much keener than any\nMay be Hired: Mercenaries, Dwarf Clansmen and Witch                human’s, and they make excellent scouts.\nHunters may hire a Dwarf Troll Slayer. Warbands that\ninclude Elves may hire Slayers, but must pay 20 gold crowns        May be Hired: Mercenaries and Sister of Sigmar may hire Elf\nafter each battle instead of 10 gold crowns. Dwarfs won’t put      Rangers. Warbands which include Dwarfs may hire Elf\nup with weak pointy-eared folk unless they have to, or are         Rangers, but must pay 40 gold crowns after each battle\nadequately compensated for their sufferance.                       instead of 20.\n\nRating: A Dwarf Troll Slayer increases the warband’s rating        Rating: An Elf Ranger increases the warband’s rating by 12\nby 12 points plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.        points plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.\n                                                                    M    WS     BS   S   T    W    I   A    LD    CL    WIL    INT\n M    WS    BS    S   T   W    I   A    LD   CL    WIL    INT       5     4     5    3    3   1    6   1     8     9     8       9\n 3     4     3    3   4    1   2   1     9    9      9      7\n                                                                   WEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:\nWEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:                                             Elf bow, sword, dagger, and Elven cloak.\nChoose from the following:\n   • Two axes                                                      SPECIAL RULES:\n   • Great Axe                                                     Race (Elf), Hired Sword, Acute Senses\n\nSPECIAL RULES:                                                     STARTING SKILL:\nRace (Dwarf), Hired Sword, Fearless                                When recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Seeker skill\n                                                                   plus one random skill from their Primary skill sets.\nSTARTING SKILL:                                                    SKILL ALLOCATION:\nWhen recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Very                Agility, (Primary), Cunning (Secondary), Shooting\nResilient skill plus one random skill from their Primary skill     (Secondary)\nsets.\nSKILL ALLOCATION:                                                  SEEKER (skill). When rolling on the Exploration chart, the\nResilience (Primary), Savagery (Secondary), Ferocity               Elf Ranger allows you to modify one dice roll by -1/+1.\n(Secondary)"},{"page":154,"text":"Fallen Knight                                                       Robber Knight\n40 gold crowns to hire +20 gold crowns upkeep.                      40 gold crowns to hire +20 gold crowns upkeep.\n\nJust as warriors of the lower social orders can become              Raubritter, or robber-knights, are unscrupulous individuals\nmercenaries, squires or nobles may offer their skills for hire by   who have turned to banditry and worse. These knights often\nbecoming a Freelancer. Some are disgraced knights, cast out by      don dark armour and have a reputation of showing their\ntheir order for minor rule breaking, or have fallen on hard         opponents no mercy. The codes of honour they once learnt\ntimes, and have come to Mordheim to seek redemption. Many           mean nothing to them now, and they will work for anyone for\nFreelancers have drifted to the shanty towns surrounding            the right coin. Many Robber Knights have drifted to the shanty\nMordheim, and offer their considerable strength for a chance        towns surrounding Mordheim, and offer their considerable\nto redeem their souls.                                              strength to the highest bidders.\n\nMay be Hired: All warbands except Orc, Skaven, the                  May be Hired: Mercenaries, Witch Hunters, The Possessed,\nPossessed, and Undead can hire a Fallen Knight.                     and Undead may hire a Robber Knight.\n\nRating: A Fallen Knight increases the warband’s rating by           Rating: A Robber Knight increases the warband’s rating by\n+20 points plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.           +20 points plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.\n M    WS    BS    S   T    W    I   A   LD    CL    WIL     INT      M    WS    BS    S   T   W    I   A   LD    CL   WIL    INT\n 4     4     3    4   3    1    4   1    8     8      7      7       4     4     3    4   3   1    4   1    8     8     7      7\nWEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:                                              WEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:\nFull Plate armour,                                                  Full Plate armour,\nPlus one of the following:                                          Plus one of the following:\n     • Sword and Shield                                                  • Sword and Shield\n     • Morning Star and Shield                                           • Morning Star and Shield\n     • Axe and Shield                                                    • Axe and Shield\n     • Hammer or mace and Shield                                         • Hammer/mace and Shield\n     • Great Sword                                                       • Great Sword\n\nSPECIAL RULES:                                                      SPECIAL RULES:\nRace (Human), Hired Sword                                           Race (Human), Hired Sword\n\nSTARTING SKILL:                                                     STARTING SKILL:\nWhen recruited the Hired Sword starts with the special rule         When recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Crushing\nCode of Honour and Resolute skill plus one random skill             Blow skill plus one random skill from their Primary skill sets.\nfrom their Secondary skill sets.\nCODE OF HONOUR If the warrior attacks an enemy                      SKILL ALLOCATION:\nwarrior on the back/rear, then they are allowed to turn             Combat (Primary), Ferocity (Secondary), Brawn (Secondary)\naround. The enemy warrior suffers no penalty for turning to\nface. The warrior will not attack a seriously wounded warrior\nand therefore will not perform a coup de grace. action\n\nSKILL ALLOCATION:\nCombat (Primary), Ferocity (Secondary), Leadership\n(Secondary)"},{"page":155,"text":"Goblin Sneak                                                     Halfling Scout\n15 gold crowns to hire +5 gold crowns upkeep.                        15 gold crowns to hire +5 gold crowns upkeep\n\nGoblins like to live in large groups and tend to be quite a cruel,   Halflings are diminutive creatures, generally more concerned\nsneaky, cowardly, and untrustworthy lot. They are neither            with the timing of their next meal (or two) than with military\nstrong nor tough, and therefore like to attack with superior         pursuits. They range from three to four feet tall, and are\nnumbers and, if possible, from a distance. As such they are          neither very strong nor tough, but are naturally good shots and\ngood archers but rather poor warriors. They steal or loot            steadfast in the face of danger. Some Halflings are more\nanything they can from anyone and destroy anything they              adventurous than others, however, and these bold spirits are\ncan't carry just for the fun of it.                                  much sought after by mercenary bands, for they are splendid\nAs can be expected, the speed and agility of Goblin Sneaks has       archers, and excellent cooks to boot.\nbeen noted by many of those who seek their fortune delving\ninto the ruins of Mordheim, Goblins make excellent bait for          May be Hired: Any warband except Orcs, Skaven, Undead\nmonster traps and are usually quick and lucky enough to              and the Possessed may hire a Halfling Scout.\nescape once the monster has fallen for the trap. Their\ndiminutive size allows them to be pushed through sewers,             Rating: A Halfling Scout increases the warband’s rating by +5\nunder badly fitting gates and into rat-infested nooks and            points plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.\ncrannies. Goblins are much sought after by mercenary bands,\nfor their sneaky ways make them good scouts.\n                                                                      M    WS    BS   S   T    W    I   A   LD    CL   WIL     INT\nMay be Hired: May be hired by any evil warband e.g. Orcs,             3     2     4   2    2   1    5   1    6     7     8      7\nSkaven, Undead and the Possessed may hire a Goblin Sneak.\n                                                                     WEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:\nRating: A Goblin Sneak increases the warband’s rating by +5          Short Bow, sword, light armour\npoints plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.\n                                                                     SPECIAL RULES:\n M    WS     BS   S    T   W    I   A    LD    CL    WIL     INT\n                                                                     Race (Halfling), Hired Sword\n 4     2     3    3    3    1   3    1    5     5      5      5\n                                                                     STARTING SKILL:\nWEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:                                               When recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Nimble skill\nShort bow, Dagger, Rope and hook.                                    plus one random skill from their Primary skill sets.\nPlus choose one of the following options:                            SKILL ALLOCATION:\n    • sword, axe, or club                                            Shooting (Primary), Agility (Secondary), Cunning\n                                                                     (Secondary)\nSPECIAL RULES:\nRace (Goblin), Hired Sword, Animosity, Fear Elves, Size\nMatters (see Orc Warband list for special rules).\n\nSTARTING SKILL:\nWhen recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Infiltrate\nskill plus one random skill from their Primary skill sets.\nSKILL ALLOCATION:\nCunning (Primary), Agility (Secondary), Shooting\n(Secondary)"},{"page":156,"text":"Halfling Thief                                                    Mercenary Wizard\n15 gold crowns to hire +5 gold crowns upkeep.                       30 gold crowns to hire +15 gold crowns upkeep\n\n\nHalflings are communal creatures, preferring to live in close-      Demand for sorcerous support always exceeds supply, so it is\nknit family groups, sharing houses, rooms, and even beds with       small wonder that enchanters both great and small make their\ndozens of friends and relatives; everyone contributes and           way to Mordheim where their talents can earn them good\neveryone shares. This interdependent lifestyle leaves many          money.\nHalflings struggling with the concept of private ownership and      These individuals have some practiced skill with magic, but\nspace.                                                              some may be rogue wizards. Perhaps they murdered their\nHalflings excel at making themselves inconspicuous. This            master, or kicked out of the College of Magic, and are now on\nprobably has something to do with their small stature,              the run, selling their abilities to the highest bidder. Others\nunassuming manner and predilection for walking around               simply can't be tied down to any single place and see the life of\nbarefooted. Whatever the reason, there are still differences in     a mercenary as a good means to expand their influence and\nindividual Halfling abilities in this area. The Halfling Thief is   study.\na master at sneakiness, sticky-fingers, and feigned innocence.      All magic is potentially dangerous and originates from Chaos,\n                                                                    so those blessed (or cursed) with the power of sorcery are hated\nMay be Hired: Any warband except Orcs and Skaven may                and feared. Still, it is not difficult to find employment if you are\nhire a Halfling Thief.                                              a Wizard, for many are willing to take the risk of persecution.\n                                                                    But hiring a Wizard does not only mean that you lose your\nRating: A Halfling Thief increases the warband’s rating by +5       gold – if the teachings of the Cult of Sigmar are to be believed,\npoints plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.               your soul is at risk as well...\n\n M    WS     BS   S   T    W    I   A    LD    CL    WIL    INT     May be Hired: Any warband except Witch Hunters and\n 3     2     4    2    2   1    5   1     6     6     8       7     Sisters of Sigmar may hire a Mercenary Wizard.\n\n\nWEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:                                              Rating: A Mercenary Wizard increases the warband’s rating\nSling, Dagger, Rope and hook, Concealed Blade                       by +16 points plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.\n\n                                                                     M    WS     BS    S   T    W    I   A    LD    CL    WIL     INT\nSPECIAL RULES:\nRace (Halfling), Hired Sword                                         4     2      2    3   3    1    3   1     7     7      8      8\n\n\nSTARTING SKILL:                                                     WEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:\nWhen recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Infiltrate           Plus choose two of the following options:\nskill plus one random skill from their Primary skill sets.              • Staff\nSKILL ALLOCATION:                                                       • Sword, axe, club, hammer or mace\nCunning (Primary), Agility (Secondary), Academic                        • Dagger\n(Secondary)\n                                                                    SPECIAL RULES:\n                                                                    Race (Human), Hired Sword, Wizard (Cast Spells Lesser\n                                                                    Magic)\n\n                                                                    Starting Spells: Mercenary Wizards start with two spells\n                                                                    generated at random from the Lesser Magic list.\n\n                                                                    STARTING SKILL:\n                                                                    When recruited the Hired Sword starts with one random skill\n                                                                    from their Primary skill sets.\n                                                                    SKILL ALLOCATION:\n                                                                    Academic (Primary), Leadership (Secondary), Combat\n                                                                    (Secondary) Or the wizard may randomly determine a new\n                                                                    spell from the Lesser Magic spell list instead."},{"page":157,"text":"Minstrel                                                Ogre Bodyguard\n20 gold crowns to hire +10 gold crowns upkeep.                    80 gold crowns to hire +30 gold crowns upkeep\n\nIn the dark and depressing streets of Mordheim a rousing tune     Ogres are large, brutish creatures, standing some ten feet tall,\nforetelling the warbands victory* can lift even the lowliest of   and all of it bone and muscle. For this reason they are much in\nspirits. A Minstrel may seem out of place in the City of the      demand as bodyguards and mercenaries, despite their lack of\nDamned but there are those who are willing to sing out their      brains. A warband backed up by an Ogre makes a fearsome\nbattle chorus for the highest bidder. These men are often         enemy, since Ogres are extremely dangerous warriors and a\nwarriors too, for only the bravest of songsters would consider    terrifying sight to behold when enraged. They happily accept\nlooking for an audience in Mordheim.                              any employer, as they are notoriously unbothered about who\n                                                                  they fight for.\nMay be Hired: Mercenaries, Sisters of Sigmar and Witch\nHunters may hire Minstrels.                                       May be Hired: Any warband except Skaven may hire an Ogre\n                                                                  Bodyguard.\nRating: A Minstrel increases the warband’s rating by +8\npoints plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.             Rating: An Ogre Bodyguard increases the warband’s rating\n                                                                  by +25 points, plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.\n M    WS    BS    S   T   W    I   A   LD    CL    WIL    INT\n 4     3     3    3   3   1    4   1    8     7     8       7      M    WS    BS    S   T   W    I   A    LD    CL   WIL     INT\n                                                                   6     3     2    4   4    3   3    2    7     5     7      4\nWEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:\nSword, Dagger                                                     WEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:\nPlus choose one of the following options:                         Light armour.\n    • Amulet of Protection                                        Plus choose one of the following options:\n    • Lucky Charm                                                     • Two of the following: sword, axe, club.\n                                                                      • Great Sword.\nSPECIAL RULES:                                                        • Great Axe.\nRace (Human), Hired Sword,                                            • Great Hammer or Club.\n\nSTARTING SKILL:                                                   SPECIAL RULES:\nWhen recruited the Hired Sword starts with the                    Race (Ogre), Hired Sword, Fear, Large Target\nInspirational skill plus one random skill from their Primary\nskill sets.                                                       STARTING SKILL:\nSKILL ALLOCATION:                                                 When recruited the Hired Sword starts with the True Grit\nLeadership (Primary), Agility (Secondary), Academic               skill plus one random skill from their Primary skill sets.\n(Secondary)                                                       SKILL ALLOCATION:\n                                                                  Brawn (Primary), Resilience (Secondary), Ferocity\nINSPIRATIONAL                                                     (Secondary)\nIf a friendly warrior within 6’’ of this warrior fails a Cool\ncheck, make a Leadership check for this warrior. If the\nLeadership check is passed, then the Cool check also counts\nas having been passed."},{"page":158,"text":"Pit Fighter                                                  Tilean marksman\n30 gold crowns to hire +15 gold crowns upkeep.                     30 gold crowns to hire +15 gold crowns upkeep.\n\n\nPit Fighters are dangerous men who make their living in the        The Empire is not the only place that breeds mercenaries. The\nillegal fighting pits of the Empire. Many of them are slaves and   constant warring among the city-states of Tilea provides many\nprisoners but some are free men who earn their living from         opportunities for a man who knows how to use a weapon. Still,\nsavage pit fights in settlements like Cutthroat’s Haven or Black   sometimes the fighting dies down in Tilea and many of these\nPit. Even though pit fights are banned in many provinces, they     mercenaries are forced to seek employment in other lands.\nare very popular and a great deal of money is wagered on the       Many of these temporarily unemployed mercenaries have\noutcome. Thus many authorities turn a blind eye to these           heard of the trouble brewing in Mordheim and have come\nbloodsports.                                                       seeking a new patron.\nWhen not in the pits, Pit Fighters offer their services to the\nhighest bidders, and they readily find employment in warbands      May be Hired: May be hired by any warband except, Skaven\nintent on exploring the ruins of Mordheim. Pit Fighters are        Undead and Orcs.\npowerful and dangerous warriors, and their unique weaponry\ngives them an advantage against almost any opponent.               Rating: A Tilean Marksman increases the warband’s rating\n                                                                   by +16 points plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.\nMay be Hired: Any warband apart from Undead and Skaven\n                                                                    M    WS    BS   S   T   W    I   A   LD    CL   WIL    INT\nmay hire a Pit Fighter.\n                                                                    4     3    4    3   3    1   3   1    7     7     7      7\nRating: A Pit Fighter increases the warband’s rating by +22\npoints, plus 1 point for each Experience point he has.             WEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:\n                                                                   Light armour, Sword, Dagger, Heavy Crossbow\n M    WS    BS    S   T   W    I   A   LD    CL    WIL    INT\n 4     4     3    4   4   1    4   2    7     7      7      7      SPECIAL RULES:\n                                                                   Race (Human), Hired Sword\nWEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT:\nMorning star, spiked gauntlet (the spiked gauntlet counts as a     STARTING SKILL:\nMain Gauche)                                                       When recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Marksman\n                                                                   skill plus one random skill from their Primary skill sets.\nSPECIAL RULES:                                                     SKILL ALLOCATION:\nRace (Human), Hired Sword                                          Shooting (Primary), Cunning (Secondary), Combat\n                                                                   (Secondary)\nSTARTING SKILL:\nWhen recruited the Hired Sword starts with the Combat\nMaster skill plus one random skill from their Primary skill\nsets.\nSKILL ALLOCATION:\nCombat (Primary), Ferocity (Secondary), Savagery\n(Secondary)"},{"page":159,"text":"Scenario rules\nHere, players will find details of the various scenario rules mentioned throughout the scenarios themselves.\nThese rules apply to all scenarios.\n\n\nPreparing for a battle\nBATTLEFIELD SET-UP                                                RANDOM SELECTION (X)\n                                                                  When a scenario instructs players to use Random Selection\nThe section that follows presents rules for setting up a          (X) to choose their starting crew, the following method is\nbattlefield and terrain, along with scenarios for warbands to     used. When Random Selection (X) is in use, there will\nfight out. These range from brief but bloody encounters           always be a number shown in brackets:\nbetween two warbands to full-scale raids onto a rival’s                • The player makes a deck of their Warrior cards.\nterritory.                                                             • The deck may not include any warriors unable to\nSome scenarios have special instructions for terrain. The                  take part in the battle.\ndefault methods are as follows:                                        • The deck may only contain one randomly selected\nThe battlefield should measure roughly 4'x4'. Larger or                    card for a warriors who have multiple Warrior\nsmaller battlefields are possible. The available terrain should            cards.\nbe divided into obstacles (any terrain measuring no more               • This deck is then shuffled and a number of cards\nthan 2\" high and no more than 2\" across) and structures or                 equal to the number shown in brackets is dealt out.\nbuildings (any terrain feature measuring more than 2\" high                 This is the player’s starting crew.\nand more than 2\" across). Players should decide between\nthem if any terrain features are difficult, dangerous or          CUSTOM SELECTION (X)\nimpassable (and therefore solid for the purposes of               When a scenario instructs players to use Custom Selection\nmeasuring and line of sight). Then, starting with the             (X) to choose their starting crew, the following method is\ndefender or the winner of a roll-off (if there is no defender),   used. Where no number is shown, there is no limit to how\nplayers take turns placing large terrain pieces anywhere on       many warriors make up the starting crew:\nthe battlefield. At any point, either player may choose to        The player chooses their crew, selecting the Warrior cards of\nplace no more. The other player may then place one final          the warriors they wish to use, equal to the number shown in\npiece of terrain.                                                 brackets.\n                                                                       • Where no number is shown in brackets, the entire\nThen, obstacles are added. Starting with the defender or the                warband may be used, should the player wish.\nwinner of a roll off (if there is no defender), players take           • If any warriors have multiple Warrior cards, the\nturns to place scatter terrain anywhere on the battlefield. At              player chooses which equipment load-out the\nany point, either player may choose to place no more. The                   warrior will use for the battle.\nother player may then place one final piece of terrain.                • The crew cannot include any warriors unable to\n                                                                            take part in the battle.\nCHOOSE CREW\nEach player chooses a starting crew for the scenario, using       Editor’s Suggestion: A warrior with Large Target trait\none of the following methods or a bespoke method, as              counts as 2 warriors for the purposes of crew selection. If\ndetermined by the scenario:                                       drawn last for random selection, return to the deck and draw\n                                                                  another warrior."},{"page":160,"text":"UNDERDOG BONUSES\nA player’s warband is classed as an Underdog if their              Ending a battle\nStarting Crew is less than their opponent’s. Where a scenario\nsays a player may draw additional Tactics cards when facing\nan opponent with a higher rating, you may choose to use            FLEEING THE BATTLEFIELD\ninstead the credit difference to buy Underdog bonuses,             In a Campaign, it is often wise for a warband to quit a fight\nwhich include hired guns up to the value of their credit           whilst they are still ahead. This is especially true in\ndifference –                                                       ambushes, and when smaller crews find themselves taking\nFor example, if a warband’s crew is 223 credits below the          on larger and more dangerous ones!\nvalue of their opponent’s, they may pay for up that value          A warband’s controlling player can choose to automatically\nfrom the following list.                                           fail any Rout test the warband is required to make.\n                                                                   Once a warband has routed (either voluntarily or by failing a\nUNDERDOG BENEFITS                       CREDIT COST                Rout test), the controlling player can declare that the\n0-5: Hire a Hired Sword                 True Cost                  warband will flee the battlefield at the start of any Action\n                                                                   phase. Any Active and Pinned warriors immediately flee the\nDEPLOYMENT                                                         battlefield.\nMany scenarios detail the size and location of deployment          Engaged warriors must attempt to break away from combat\nzones. For those that do not, the standard methods are as          – make an Initiative check for each. If the check is passed,\nfollows                                                            they flee the battlefield. If the check is failed, they are\nDraw a line dividing the battlefield into two equal halves.        Seriously Injured.\nStarting with the defender, or the winner of a roll-off,           Once the warband that has declared it will flee has only\nplayers select one half of the battlefield in which to set their   Seriously Injured warriors on the battlefield, the battle ends.\ncrews up. The defender/winner of the roll-off sets up first.       Roll to see if the Seriously Injured warriors succumb to their\nAll warriors in both players’ crews must be set up at least 12\"    injuries during the Wrap-up as usual.\naway from the dividing line.\n                                                                   LEAVING THE BATTLEFIELD\n                                                                   In certain scenarios, warriors leave the battlefield\n                                                                   voluntarily. Warriors removed from the board by escaping\n                                                                   as part of these scenarios are ignored for the purposes of\n                                                                   making Rout Tests (i.e. they do not count as part of the crew\n\n\n                                                                   Campaign rewards\n                                                                   Each scenario provides rewards, such as experience, credits\n                                                                   or reputation for the warbands playing it. At the end of the\n                                                                   game players should consult the scenario and see if they\n                                                                   qualify for any of its rewards, adding them to their warband\n                                                                   rosters if they do. When using any of the scenarios in this\n                                                                   book, some of the rewards might need to be altered\n                                                                   depending on the type of campaign being played."},{"page":161,"text":"Special scenario rules\nThe following rules apply only for certain scenarios.\n\nREINFORCEMENTS                                                        •    Once the sentry has moved, the player who moved\nIf a warband has Reinforcements available, the scenario will               them will decide which way they are facing.\ndescribe how to create a Reinforcements deck from the\nWarrior cards not in their starting crew:                         Sentries may open or close any door within 1\" of them\n     • Reinforcements always arrive at the end of the End         before or after making their Move action.\n         phase. The scenario will state the round(s) on           SPOTTING ATTACKERS\n         which reinforcements arrive and how many arrive.         After a sentry has moved, roll 2D6. If there are any attackers\n     • Reinforcements are randomly drawn from the                 within that many inches of any sentry and within their\n         Reinforcements deck and a D6 rolled. On a 3+, the        vision arc, they may be spotted. Roll a D6 and consult the\n         controlling player deploys them. On a 1-2, the           table below, subtracting 1 from the result if the attacker is\n         enemy player deploys them.                               Prone:\n     • Unless instructed otherwise by the scenario,\n         Reinforcements are deployed on any battlefield           Attacker Position                                  Dice Roll\n         edge, not within 6\" of an enemy warrior.                 Within 3\", regardless of the sentry’s facing       2+\n                                                                  In the open, regardless of the sentry’s facing     2+\nFAQ 2019: Q. When Reinforcements arrive, does their               Partial cover and more than 3\"                     4+\npresence count towards a Rout Test?                               Full cover and more than 3\"                        6+\nA. No. When making a Rout Test it is only the number of\nwarriors in your Starting Crew that matter, regardless of how     If the sentry is not Engaged, make an Initiative check for the\nmany Reinforcements have since turned up.                         sentry. If it is passed, they can immediately make a ranged\n                                                                  attack against the spotted enemy warrior.\nSNEAK ATTACKS\nIn some scenarios, the defender may not initially be aware of     RAISING THE ALARM\nthe presence of the attacker. In these scenarios, the following   The alarm is raised if:\nrules are used:                                                       • Any sentries have a Ready marker when it is the\nSENTRIES                                                                  defender’s turn to activate a warrior.\nSentries are deployed as described in the scenarios. Sentries         • If an attacker fires a ranged weapon, roll 2D6 and\nnever gain Ready markers before the alarm is raised:                      add the weapon’s Strength after the attack has been\n     • When it is the defender’s turn to activate a warrior,              resolved. If the weapon has the Blast trait, add 2 to\n          they shuffle together the Warrior cards for their               the result. If the total is 10 or more, the alarm is\n          sentries into a deck and randomly draw one,                     raised.\n          returning it after the activation is complete. Note         • From the fifth round onwards, if the attacker rolls a\n          that a sentry can be activated several times in the             1 or 2 for their Priority roll, the alarm is raised.\n          same turn in this way.                                  Once the alarm is raised, all sentries immediately gain Ready\n     • Once the attacker has activated their last warrior,        markers, the Sentries rules are no longer used, and the\n          the defender activates one more sentry and then the     defender’s warriors follow all the normal rules.\n          Activation phase ends.\n     • When a sentry is activated, both players roll a D6. If     HOME TURF ADVANTAGE\n          the rolls are the same, the sentry turns to face a      If a scenario indicates that one warband has the Home Turf\n          random direction determined by a scatter dice and       Advantage, they gain the following benefits:\n          the defending player makes one Move (Simple)                  • When making a Rout test, roll 2D6 and discard the\n          action with them. If the rolls are not the same, the              highest.\n          player who rolled the highest can make one Move               • Warriors add 1 to the result of any Rally tests.\n          (Simple) action with the sentry."},{"page":162,"text":"Scenario 1: Skirmish\nIn the vastness of the Mordheim ruins there is always the risk of running into a rival warband. While two groups sometimes pass each\nother without a fight, more often than not there is a vicious battle amongst the ruins. If a warband can drive their rivals away, they\nwill have a larger area in which to search for wyrdstone.\n\nBATTLEFIELD                                                          VICTORY\nThis scenario uses the standard Battlefield Set-up rules.            If one warband has warriors on the battlefield at the end of\n                                                                     the battle, they are victorious. If neither warband has\nCREWS                                                                warriors, the battle ends in a draw.\nThis scenario uses the standard rules for choosing a crew.\nBoth players use the Custom Selection (10) method.                   EXPERIENCE\n                                                                          •   Each warrior that took part in the battle earns 1\nDEPLOYMENT                                                                    XP.\nThis scenario uses the standard rules for deployment.                     •   The Leader of the warband that scored the most\n                                                                              points gains 1 XP (regardless of whether they\n                                                                              took part in the battle or not). – in the case of a\nOBJECTIVES\n                                                                              draw, neither Leader gets this bonus.\nEach warband’s aim is simple: take out as many of the\nenemy as possible! Each warband scores points for each\nopposing warrior that goes Out of Action or flees the                REPUTATION\nbattlefield. They score:                                                  •   The warband that scored the most points gains 2\n          3 points for a Leader,                                              Reputation.\n          2 points for a Champion and                                     •   Each warband gains 1 Reputation if this was their\n          1 point for any other warrior.                                      first battle against this opponent.\n                                                                          •   If either warband routed, they lose 1 Reputation.\nENDING THE BATTLE\nIf either warband has no warriors left on the board at the\nend of any round, the battle ends immediately."},{"page":163,"text":"Scenario 2: Wyrdstone hunt\nScattered in the ruins of Mordheim are innumerable tiny shards of priceless wyrdstone. It often happens that two warbands come\nupon the same area and only a battle can determine who will pick the spoils. In this scenario, warbands encounter each other\nwhile scavenging in the same ruined warehouse, vault, temple or other such potentially rich building.\n\nBATTLEFIELD                                                             •   A warrior can carry any amount of wyrdstone\nThis scenario uses the standard Battlefield Set-up rules.                   counters without any penalty.\n                                                                        •   Warriors cannot transfer their wyrdstone\nCREWS                                                                       counters to another warrior.\nThis scenario uses the standard rules for choosing a crew.              •   If the warrior who is carrying a counter is taken\nThe attacker uses the Custom Selection method, while the                    out of action, place the counter on the table\ndefender uses the Random Selection (D3+4) method.                           where he fell.\nAfter the defender has dealt out their starting crew, the\nrest of their deck is set to one side to form their                ENDING THE BATTLE\nReinforcements deck                                                If either warband has no warriors left on the board at the\n                                                                   end of any round, the battle ends immediately.\nDEPLOYMENT\nOnce you have placed the terrain, place D3+2 Wyrdstone             FLEEING THE BATTLEFIELD\ncounters on the tabletop to represent where the shards are.        If a player voluntarily routs and flees the battlefield, the\nRoll a D6 to see which player places a counter first. Each         opposing player automatically wins the scenario and may\nplayer takes it in turn to place a counter. The counters           claim all the wyrdstone counters.\nmust be placed more than 10\" from the edge of the table\nand at least 6\" away from each other.                              VICTORY\nNote that counters are placed before deciding which edge           A warband wins if, at the end of the battle, they have\nthe warbands will play from, so it is a good idea to put           claimed more wyrdstone counters than their opponent.\ncounters towards the middle of the table.\nEach player rolls a dice. Whoever rolls highest chooses            EXPERIENCE\nwhich table edge to set up on, placing all his warriors                 •   Each warrior that took part in the battle earns 1\nwithin 8\" of that edge. His opponent then sets up within 8\"                 XP.\nof the opposite edge.                                                   •   +1 XP per Wyrdstone Counter. If a warrior is\n                                                                            carrying a Wyrdstone counter at the end of the\nOBJECTIVES                                                                  battle he receives +1 Experience.\nBoth warbands are attempting to secure as much                          •   The Leader of the warband that scored the most\nwyrdstone as possible.                                                      points gains 1 XP (regardless of whether they\n                                                                            took part in the battle or not). – in the case of a\nCLAIMING WYRDSTONE AND ESCAPING                                             draw, neither Leader gets this bonus.\n    •    If a warrior is within 1\" of a wyrdstone counter,\n         they may make a Move (simple) action, carrying            REPUTATION\n         the wyrdstone counter with them. When the                      •   Each warband gains 1 Reputation if this was their\n         action ends, the counter is placed in base contact                 first battle against this opponent.\n         with this warrior.                                             •   Each warband gains 1 Reputation for each\n    •    Any model with the Warbeasts special rule                          wyrdstone counter that they claimed.\n         cannot pick up counters."},{"page":164,"text":"Scenario 3: Ambush\n                              A warband sets a trap for their rivals, hoping to catch them unawares...\n\nATTACKER AND DEFENDER                                                 activate that many warriors before the defender activates\nIn this scenario, one warband is the attacker and the other           their first warrior.\nis the defender. In a campaign, the player who chose this             Additionally, during the first round, make an INT check\nscenario is the attacker. In a skirmish, players roll off and         for each warrior when they are activated. If the check is\nthe winner decides whether they will attack or defend.                failed, the warrior can only make one action this round\n                                                                      instead of two. (The INT check is taken by all warriors,\nBATTLEFIELD                                                           even those that are Immune to psychology, as it represents\nThis scenario uses the standard Battlefield Set-up rules,             how quickly they react to the surprise attack. Those with\nwith the following exceptions:                                        slower wits react slower).\nNo large terrain can be placed within 12\" of the centre of\nthe battlefield. Scatter terrain can still be placed in this          SLIPPING THE NET\narea, however in this scenario the defender may place all of          Any defending warrior that is within 1’’ of an edge of the\nthe scatter terrain. The defender’s deployment zone is                battlefield at the start of the End phase can escape if their\nanywhere within 6\" of the centre of the battlefield                   controlling player wishes, even if they are Seriously\n                                                                      Injured. They are removed from the battlefield, and count\nCREWS                                                                 as being Out of Action from now on for the purposes of\nThis scenario uses the standard rules for choosing a crew.            Rout tests. In a campaign, each defender who escapes in\nThe attacker uses the Custom Selection method, while the              this way earns 1 XP.\ndefender uses the Random Selection (D3+5) method.\n                                                                      ENDING THE BATTLE\nDEPLOYMENT                                                            If either warband has no warriors left on the board at the\nThe defender goes through the warriors in their crew one              end of any round, the battle ends immediately.\nat a time, in an order of their choosing, rolling a D6 for\neach. On 1-5, they set up that warrior in their deployment            VICTORY\nzone. On a 6, the warrior is set aside.                               The attacker wins if, at the end of the battle, more\nThe attacker then sets up their crew anywhere on the                  defenders were taken Out of Action than escaped –\nbattlefield. Attacking warriors must be set up at least 12\"           defenders that fled the battlefield do not count. Otherwise,\naway from any defenders, or at least 6\" away from any                 the defender wins.\ndefenders if no defenders can draw a line of sight to them.\nAfter the attackers have set up, the defender sets up any of          EXPERIENCE\ntheir warriors who were previously set aside. These                      • Each warrior that took part in the battle earns 1\nwarriors can be set up anywhere on the battlefield, as long                 XP.\nas they are not Engaged with an enemy warrior.                           • If none of the defenders escaped, the attacker’s\n                                                                            Leader earns 1 XP (regardless of whether they\nOBJECTIVES                                                                  took part in the battle).\nThe attackers are attempting to take out as many of the\ndefenders as possible; the defenders are trying to either             REPUTATION\nreturn the favour, or escape.                                            • If more defenders went Out of Action than\n                                                                            escaped, the attackers gain 1 Reputation. If more\nSURPRISE ATTACK                                                             defenders escaped than went Out of Action, the\nThe attackers take the Priority marker in the first round of                defenders gain 2 Reputation.\nthe game. In addition, before activating their first warrior             • If either warband routed, they lose 1 Reputation.\nin the first round, the attacker rolls a D3 – they can"},{"page":165,"text":"Scenario 4: Looters\n                  A warband launches an all-out attack on a rival’s home turf in the hope of raiding their stash.\n\nATTACKER AND DEFENDER                                                 ENDING THE BATTLE\nIn this scenario, one warband is the attacker and the other           If either warband has no warriors left on the board at the\nis the defender. In a campaign, the player who chose this             end of any round, the battle ends immediately.\nscenario is the attacker. In a skirmish, players roll off and\nthe winner decides whether they will attack or defend.                FLEEING THE BATTLEFIELD\n                                                                      If the defender voluntarily routs and flees the battlefield,\nBATTLEFIELD                                                           the attacker automatically wins the scenario and may\nThis scenario uses the standard Battlefield Set-up rules.             claim all the loot counters.\n\nCREWS                                                                 VICTORY\nThis scenario uses the standard rules for choosing a crew.            The attacker wins if, at the end of the battle, they have\nThe attacker uses the Custom Selection method, while the              claimed more counters than there are left on the\ndefender uses the Random Selection (D3+4) method.                     battlefield. Otherwise, the defender wins.\nAfter the defender has dealt out their starting crew, the\nrest of their deck is set to one side to form their                   CREDITS\nReinforcements deck                                                   Roll a D3 for each casket that was claimed by the attackers,\n                                                                      and multiply the result by 10. That many credits are\nHOME TURF ADVANTAGE                                                   moved from the defender’s Stash to the attacker’s Stash\nThe defender has the Home Turf Advantage.                             (note that the attacker's gains cannot exceed the defender’s\n                                                                      losses).\nDEPLOYMENT\nThe defender places up four loot counters anywhere on                 EXPERIENCE\nthe battlefield. Then they set up their crew. Each warrior               • Each warrior that took part in the battle earns 1\nmust be within 3\" of one of the loot counters.                              XP.\nThe attacker then picks one edge of the battlefield – this is            • If at least two counters were claimed by the\ntheir escape route. They set up their entire crew within 6\"                 attackers, their Leader then earns 1 XP\nof that edge.                                                               (regardless of whether they took part in the battle\n                                                                            or not).\nOBJECTIVES\nThe attackers are attempting to secure the four loot                  REPUTATION\ncounters and move them off via their escape route. The                   • Each warband gains 1 Reputation if this was their\ndefenders are attempting to stop them.                                      first battle against this opponent.\n                                                                         • The attackers gain 1 Reputation for each loot\nCLAIMING LOOT AND ESCAPING                                                  casket that they claimed. The defenders gain 1\nIf one of the four loot counters is within 1\" of the escape                 Reputation for each loot casket still on the\nroute at the start of any End phase, it is claimed and                      battlefield when the battle ends. If all four loot\nremoved from the battlefield. In addition, any attacking                    counters were claimed by the attacker, the\nwarriors within 1\" of the escape route at the start of any                  defenders lose 2 Reputation; if all four were still\nEnd phase can escape (even if they are Seriously Injured) if                on the battlefield, the attackers lose 2 Reputation.\nthe controlling player wishes – they are removed from the\nbattlefield and count as being Out of Action from now on\nfor the purposes of Rout tests.\nIf the attacking warband routs, they can no longer claim\nloot counters, but warriors can still escape.\n\nREINFORCEMENTS\nAt the start of each End phase, the defender receives\nReinforcements. One random warrior arrives at the start\nof the first End phase, two at the start of the second End\nphase, and so on."},{"page":166,"text":"Scenario 5: The Heist\n                            A warband sneaks into a rival’s territory, hoping to steal their loot and money.\n\nATTACKER AND DEFENDER                                                 STEALING THE LOOT\nThis scenario uses the Sneak Attack Rules. In this scenario, one         • If a warrior is within 1\" of a Loot counter, they may\nwarband is the attacker and the other is the defender. In a                 make a Move (simple) action, carrying the Loot\ncampaign, the player who chose this scenario is the attacker. In            counter with them. When the action ends, the counter\na skirmish, players roll off and the winner decides whether                 is placed in base contact with this warrior.\nthey will attack or defend.                                              • Any model with the Warbeasts special rule cannot\n                                                                            pick up counters.\nBATTLEFIELD                                                              • A warrior can carry any amount of Loot counters\nThe defender places a small piece of terrain, which should be at            without any penalty.\nleast 2\" square, to represent the attacker’s Objective. This could       • Warriors cannot transfer their Loot counters to\nbe a small building, a shed, a stack of crates and barrels, a cart          another warrior.\nwith goods on it, etc. It is recommended that this is set up as          • If the warrior who is carrying a counter is taken out of\nclose to the centre of the battlefield as possible. Place D3 loot           action, place the counter on the table where he fell.\ncounters on the Objective as well. Then, the battlefield is set up\nusing the standard Battlefield Set-up rules.                          TAKING FLIGHT\n                                                                      Once the attackers have picked up a Loot counter, they can\nDESIGNER'S NOTE: ADDING SOME FLAVOUR                                  disappear back into the shadows. Any attacking warrior that is\nThis scenario is a good opportunity to put together some              within 1’’ of an edge of the battlefield at the start of the End\nthemed terrain, based on the territory that the attackers are         phase can take flight if the controlling player wishes, even if\ntargeting. For example, players could create a Vampire’s lair,        they are Seriously Injured. They are removed from the\na Mercenary’s tavern, etc. This location is the home base for\n                                                                      battlefield, and count as being Out of Action from now on for\nthe defender.\n                                                                      the purposes of Rout tests.\nCREWS\n                                                                      REINFORCEMENTS\nThe attacker uses the Custom Selection method to choose their\n                                                                      In the round that the alarm is raised, and in each subsequent\ncrew. The defender’s starting crew are their sentries. They use\n                                                                      round, D6 Reinforcements arrive until the entire warband has\nthe Random Selection (5) method, but before drawing their\n                                                                      arrive.\ncrew, they can declare any number of warriors unfit (or too\nimportant) for sentry duty, removing them from their deck –\nthey are returned to the deck after the starting crew has been        ENDING THE BATTLE\ndrawn, then the rest of the deck is set to one side to form their     If either warband has no warriors left on the board at the end\nReinforcements deck.                                                  of any round, the battle ends immediately.\n\n\nHOME TURF ADVANTAGE                                                   VICTORY\nThe defender has the Home Turf Advantage.                             The attacker wins if, at the end of the battle, they have taken all\n                                                                      the Loot Counters off the board and fewer than half of their\n                                                                      crew have been taken Out of Action. If they have not taken any\nDEPLOYMENT\n                                                                      Loot Counters off the board, the defender wins. Otherwise, the\nThe defender sets up their sentries anywhere on the battlefield.\n                                                                      battle ends in a draw.\nThe attacker then sets up each of their warriors within 2’’ of\nany edge of the battlefield, and if free to use all edges.\n                                                                      REWARDS\n                                                                      For each Loot Counter the attacker takes off the board roll a\nOBJECTIVES\n                                                                      D6:\nThe attackers are attempting to steal the loot counters. The\ndefenders are trying to stop this at all costs!\n                                                                      D6      Result\n                                                                      1-2-    The Loot Casket contains D3x10 gold crowns\n                                                                      3-4     The Loot Casket contains D6x10 gold crowns\n                                                                      5-6     The Loot casket contains one item from the\n                                                                              defenders stash (Roll a D6: 1-3 defender chooses\n                                                                              the item, 4-6 the attacker chooses the item)."},{"page":167,"text":"EXPERIENCE                                                           REPUTATION\n   • Each warrior that took part in the battle earns 1 XP.              • Each warband gains 1 Reputation if this was their first\n   • If the target was destroyed, the attackers Leader earns               battle against this opponent.\n      1 XP (regardless of whether they took part in the                 • If the attackers managed to take a Loot Counter off\n      battle or not).                                                      the board, their Reputation increases by 1 per\n                                                                           counter, and the defender’s Reputation decreases by 1\n                                                                           (total).\n                                                                        • If either warband routed, they lose 1 Reputation.\n\n\n\n\n                                  Scenario 6: Rescue Mission\n                     With one of their number in the hands of their rivals, a warband launches a daring rescue...\n\nATTACKER AND DEFENDER                                                FREE CAPTIVE (BASIC) – Pick a Captive within 1\" and roll a\nThis scenario uses the Sneak Attack rules. In this scenario, one     D6. If the result is a 3 or higher, the Captive is freed. On a 1 or\nwarband is the attacker and the other is the defender. In a          2 however, the Captive is not freed from their shackles and\ncampaign, the player who chose this scenario is the attacker. In     remains Captive. In a campaign, the warrior who frees a\na skirmish, players roll off and the winner decides whether          Captive gains D3 XP.\nthey will attack or defend.                                          In addition, in the End phase of each round, the attacking\n                                                                     player may declare that one or more Captives will attempt to\nTHE TARGET                                                           escape their bonds. For each Captive that does so, roll 2D6 and\nIn a campaign, this scenario can only be played if one or more       add their Strength. On a 13 or more, they break loose and are\nof the attacker’s warriors has been captured by the defender.        freed; on a 6 or less, however, they injure themselves and suffer\nThese warriors are the Captives. In a skirmish battle, the           a Flesh Wound.\nattacker picks one of their warriors at random to act as the         Freed Captives gain a Ready marker in the following Priority\nCaptive.                                                             phase and can be activated as normal from then on. Their\n                                                                     rescuers immediately return their equipment to them.\nBATTLEFIELD\nThis scenario uses the standard Battlefield Set-up rules.            HOME TURF ADVANTAGE\n                                                                     The defender has the Home Turf Advantage.\nDESIGNER'S NOTE: ADDING SOME FLAVOUR\nThis scenario is a good opportunity to put together some             DEPLOYMENT\nthemed terrain, based on the territory that the attackers are        The defender sets up their sentries and Captive(s) anywhere on\ntargeting. For example, players could create a Vampire’s lair,       the battlefield. The attacker then sets up each of their warriors\na Mercenary’s tavern, etc. This location is the home base for        within 2\" of any edge of the battlefield, and is free to use all\nthe defender.                                                        edges.\n\nCREWS\n                                                                     OBJECTIVES\nThe attacker uses the Custom Selection method to choose their\n                                                                     The attackers are attempting to rescue the Captive(s) –\ncrew. The defender’s starting crew are their sentries. They use\n                                                                     preferably without the defender noticing.\nthe Random Selection (5) method, but before drawing their\ncrew, they can declare any number of warriors unfit (or too\n                                                                     TAKING FLIGHT\nimportant) for sentry duty, removing them from their deck –\n                                                                     Once the attackers have freed their Captive(s) they can\nthey are returned to the deck after the starting crew has been\n                                                                     disappear back into the shadows. Any attacking warrior that is\ndrawn, then the rest of the deck is set to one side to form their\n                                                                     within 1’’ of any edge of the battlefield at the start of the End\nReinforcements deck.\n                                                                     phase can take flight if the controlling player wishes, even if\n                                                                     they are Seriously injured. They are removed from the\nCAPTIVE WARRIORS\n                                                                     battlefield, and count as being Out of Action from now on for\nCaptive warriors never gain Ready markers, and cannot be\n                                                                     the purposes of Rout tests.\nactivated or attacked by either player.\nNon-Captive attackers can make the following action:"},{"page":168,"text":"REINFORCEMENTS                                                       VICTORY\nIn the round that the alarm is raised, and in each subsequent        The attacker wins if, at the end of the battle, all of their\nround, D6 Reinforcements arrive until the entire warband has         warriors held Captive were freed and have taken flight. If no\narrived.                                                             Captives took flight, the defender wins. Otherwise, the game is\n                                                                     a draw.\nENDING THE BATTLE                                                    EXPERIENCE\nIf either warband has no warriors left on the board at the end            • Each warrior that took part in the battle earns 1 XP.\nof any round, the battle ends immediately.                                • If a Captive is freed and takes flight, they earn 1 XP.\n                                                                          • If all of the Captives were freed and took flight, the\nFLEEING THE BATTLEFIELD                                                       attacker’s Leader earns 1 XP (regardless of whether\nIf the defender voluntarily routs and flees the battlefield, their            they took part in the battle or not).\nopponent automatically wins the scenario and the Captive is\nFreed.                                                               REPUTATION\n                                                                        • For each Captive that was freed, the attacker gains 2\n                                                                           Reputation and the defender loses 1 Reputation.\n                                                                        • If either warband routed, they lose 1 Reputation."},{"page":169,"text":"Blank warrior card:\n\n  NAME:\n  TYPE:\n\n   M      WS   BS   S   T       W         I    A   LD       CL WIL INT\n\n\n\n Weapon                 Range       Str   AP   D   Traits\n\n\n\n\n    SKILLS:\n    WARGEAR:"},{"page":170,"text":"NOTES ON CHARACTERISTICS\n                                 AND COST\n    •   Warrior Characteristics are written like Warhammer/Mordheim. It was just easier to convert profiles over\n        and might be more familiar to Mordheim players.\n    •   LD, INT, CL, WP can be converted to Necromunda characteristics if you wish, they are basically inverted.\n    •   To calculate the cost of warriors I used the Necromunda Gang War supplement (page 16). I took this base\n        profile for a human:\n\n        M        WS         BS           S   T      W         I        A       LD       CL      WIL       INT\n        4         3          3           3   3      1         3        1        7        7       7         7\n\nBasic Human cost = 50 Gold Crowns\n\nThen compared with the profile of the warrior I was calculating cost for. I added or subtracted the following:\n\nAdjustment                                                  Cost in Gold Crowns\nWP or INT                                                   5\nLD or CL                                                    10\nInitiative                                                  10\nMovement                                                    10\nWS or BS                                                    20\nStrength or Toughness                                       30\nWounds or Attacks                                           45\nEach starting skill or special ability                      10 to 20\n\nMany of the profiles I took from Warhammer Fantasy Battle Rulebook 3rd edition as it had the LD, CL, WP, INT\ncharacteristics.\n\nWhen it came to deciding what characteristic increases the Heroes (Leaders and Champions) should have, I first\nbased it on the Necromunda gangs (increased wounds and attacks etc). Non-magic users had LD and CL increased,\nand magic users had INT and WP increased.\n\nSkills and abilities (e.g. special rules) could vary in cost. How good is the ability? Is it positive or negative? For\ninstance, the special rule Fear can be quite powerful, but does it cost more than Unbreakable? Stupidity is a negative\nability, but how much should you reduce the cost of a Rat Ogre or Troll?\nLike so many of these games balancing is difficult. I would also check Warhammer armies and WFB rulebook 3rd\nedition to see how much the points cost differed from a base human. A skeleton warrior in many editions is double\nthe cost of a base human.\n\nIt’s not an exact science. I’ve tried to use common sense to be fair. Even large games companies have trouble\nbalancing points costs."},{"page":171,"text":"• NOTES\n•   Pinned is used a lot less in Mordheim. I thought about removing it entirely, but there are a few skills and\n    abilities which use it, plus it still suits Black Powder weapons. There are less missile weapons in Mordheim\n    than Necromunda, and nearly all Necromunda warriors are armed with a gun whereas many warriors in\n    Mordheim are not. Many of the missile weapons in Mordheim are bows or crossbows which aren’t like\n    bullets. The Pinned rule in Necromunda sort of represents supressing fire, or semi-automatic weapons,\n    which isn’t really a thing in a medieval skirmish game. Blackpowder weapons use Pinned, but these are only\n    used by a few warbands, and the slow reload balances out the advantage of the Pinned rule.\n•   Stun trait for weapons. I tried to fit this in, but the rules became messy. It is hard to fit in as warriors are now\n    seriously wounded instead. It was maybe a bit overpowered anyway.\n•   Helmets. I like the idea of helmets, but there is no need for them as there is no Stun trait. On the plus side,\n    you don’t need to worry about your models having helmets now.\n•   Added magic rules. These are similar to the Psyker rules, includes Magic Miscasts and Dispels.\n•   Added Psychology rules- Fear, Stupidity Frenzy, Hatred. Fear and psychology play a big part in Mordheim so\n    some skills were changed to reflect this.\n•   Added Special Rules- such as Large Target, Undead, Stubborn etc.\n•   Hiding- I had this in the rules for a long time. Removed as is just felt forced. I like the idea of Hiding and\n    warriors sneaking. Skills like Sneak up (run and hide) Acute Senses (spotting distance x2) and equipment like\n    lanterns and torches, which are on some of the models so they seem important. Some scenarios use\n    sneaking and hiding instead.\n•   The skills are almost the same as Necromunda, some have been renamed to something sounding more\n    fantasy (e.g., Medicae becomes Healer). There is less chance of becoming Pinned and no ammo checks in\n    Mordheim, so some skills were changed to reflect that.\n•   Removed All Alone tests. I like the idea of All Alone tests, but there are now Panic tests instead. Having both\n    was too much. Panic gives the impression that warriors care about other warband members and aren’t just\n    mindless bots. When a warband member dies near them they might run away!\n•   Replaced Mordheim injury table with Necromunda one. This removes knocked downed and Stunned. When\n    a warrior is Knocked Down, they are placed face up, and when a warrior is Pinned, they are placed up so this\n    conflicts with each other. The Necromunda injury table is more streamlined and it was easier to convert\n    everything over. On a positive side there are now flesh wounds, and equipment and skills which can negate\n    them.\n•   No critical hits- some weapon traits use natural 6s to hit so critical hits cannot be used.\n•   No intercepts.\n•   Shooting- No penalty for Move and fire. But there is an aim action instead of moving.\n•   Henchmen are no longer in groups, they are individuals. For all intents and purposes, they are like\n    Necromuda gangers."}],"headings":[{"page":3,"title":"Part one: the rules"},{"page":4,"title":"SKILLS:                ~ 1:11.\\T' 5"},{"page":22,"title":"skills and traits may modify this."},{"page":28,"title":"SKILLS: When recruited, a Champion may choose one skill                 trait, each unsaved wound is doubled. So, for example, if an"},{"page":32,"title":"spells will not protect Prayers. And priests can wear armour"},{"page":32,"title":"Spells (X)’ actions, which enable them to manifest their"},{"page":35,"title":"Prayers of Sigmar"},{"page":40,"title":"Prayers of Ulric"},{"page":41,"title":"Prayers of Taal"},{"page":41,"title":"Prayers of Taal work like Prayers of Sigmar although the Taal Priest never wears armour."},{"page":43,"title":"Weapons And Armour"},{"page":56,"title":"prayers from such a book, strengthening his faith and"},{"page":59,"title":"spells or the Lesser Magic list. See the Magic section for"},{"page":60,"title":"Campaign play, Animals do not gain XP for rallying in this way)."},{"page":62,"title":"Skills: Bull Charge, Bear Hug (use Headbutt Skill)                 Warbands may only purchase Giant Wolves if they have a"},{"page":62,"title":"Skills: Bull Charge                                                SPECIAL RULES:"},{"page":69,"title":"Skills"},{"page":74,"title":"CAMPAIGN PLAY                                                       WEAPONS AND WARGEAR"},{"page":74,"title":"SKIRMISH PLAY                                                       from a general warband list."},{"page":76,"title":"Campaign play. However, if a warband has been founded for         • Raw Recruits can be recruited also, changing their type to"},{"page":76,"title":"Skirmish play, any unspent Gold Crowns are simply lost.           Henchmen."},{"page":127,"title":"Founding warbands"},{"page":130,"title":"SKILLS"},{"page":130,"title":"Skills can be obtained by Leader, Champions, and Raw Recruits (as well as Henchmen who have become Veterans). The skills that"},{"page":140,"title":"spells or close combat attacks while ethereal. Conversely,                    becomes the priority target."},{"page":141,"title":"skills and magical weapons, and have the Always Strikes Last          the original."},{"page":141,"title":"spells.)                                                              Magic table. Any warrior with this item can perform the action"},{"page":150,"title":"Hired Swords"},{"page":150,"title":"hired swords and experience"},{"page":169,"title":"SKILLS:"},{"page":170,"title":"Skills and abilities (e.g. special rules) could vary in cost. How good is the ability? Is it positive or negative? For"}],"rulesMeta":[["Rules core","Warriors, characteristics, actions, movement, shooting, close combat, injury and psychology"],["Campaign","Campaign sequence, injuries, trading, income, recruitment, hired swords and advancement"],["Weapons & Wargear","Weapon types, armour, equipment, trading and weapon traits"],["Skills","All skill descriptions and skill sets"],["Magic & Prayers","Spells, prayers, wizards, priests and magical rules"],["Warband Lists","All faction creation rules, warrior entries, skill access and faction equipment lists"],["Exploration","Exploration tables, income, events, treasures and campaign tables"],["Hired Swords","Hired Sword rules and individual entries"],["Scenarios","Scenario rules and scenario-specific content"]],"priceChartSourcePages":[148,149]};
